# figma-gotchas

**Version**: v2.2 (session 2026-05-21)

_Examples below reference the original {{SYSTEM_NAME}} system; the patterns are system-agnostic._

Hard-won lessons working with the {{SYSTEM_NAME}} design system in Figma. Read this BEFORE writing `use_figma` code. The Plugin-API traps (Gotchas #20–#29) are universal — they bite any Figma design-system work regardless of the system.

## Critical universals

### Gotcha #1 — Async API everywhere
- `page.loadAsync()` mandatory before reading children of any page that isn't `figma.currentPage`
- Font loading mandatory before ANY text op (creation, edit, style binding, swapComponent on instances with text)
- Required fonts (project-specific — see {{FONTS}}): Work Sans (Bold, SemiBold, Medium, Regular, ExtraBold) + Inter (Regular, Medium, Semi Bold, Bold)

```javascript
await Promise.all([
  figma.loadFontAsync({ family: "Work Sans", style: "Bold" }),
  figma.loadFontAsync({ family: "Work Sans", style: "SemiBold" }),
  figma.loadFontAsync({ family: "Work Sans", style: "Medium" }),
  figma.loadFontAsync({ family: "Work Sans", style: "Regular" }),
  figma.loadFontAsync({ family: "Work Sans", style: "ExtraBold" }),
  figma.loadFontAsync({ family: "Inter", style: "Regular" }),
  figma.loadFontAsync({ family: "Inter", style: "Medium" }),
  figma.loadFontAsync({ family: "Inter", style: "Semi Bold" }),
  figma.loadFontAsync({ family: "Inter", style: "Bold" }),
]);
```

**Note**: Inter style is `"Semi Bold"` (with space), not `"SemiBold"`. Same for `"Extra Bold"` vs `"ExtraBold"`.

### Gotcha #2 — No async helpers inside use_figma
Top-level await only. Inline everything. Don't define `async function` helpers — they break execution.

### Gotcha #3 — figma.currentPage assignment
Setting `figma.currentPage = page` is not supported. Use `await figma.setCurrentPageAsync(page)` instead.

### Gotcha #4 — Library publishing is UI-only
Plugin API cannot publish a library. Designer must click "Publish Library" in Figma UI. After publish:
- Consumer files can import new content via `importComponentByKeyAsync` / `importStyleByKeyAsync` / `variables.importVariableByKeyAsync`
- Existing instances in consumer files do NOT auto-refresh layout (see Gotcha #20)

### Gotcha #5 — Plugin data API
- `getPluginData` / `setPluginData` are web-only and need manifest IDs
- Use `getSharedPluginData(namespace, key)` / `setSharedPluginData(namespace, key, value)` instead
- Pick a stable namespace (≥3 chars, alphanumeric/_/.)

## Component & variant gotchas

### Gotcha #6 — Remote components can't be renamed or reparented
A component imported from a library is read-only in terms of name + parent. To "localize":
1. `importComponentByKeyAsync(key)` to get the main component
2. `mainComponent.createInstance()` to make an instance
3. `instance.detachInstance()` to detach
4. `figma.createComponentFromNode(detachedFrame)` to create a new local component
5. Optionally `figma.combineAsVariants([components])` to merge into a set

### Gotcha #7 — Never .remove() source rectangles during component creation
When wrapping a node in a component, use `node.parent = newComponent` (or `appendChild`) to MOVE the node, not `node.remove()` + recreate.

### Gotcha #8 — Adding new variants to a set
To add Size=XL or similar to an existing component_set:
1. `existingVariant.clone()` to duplicate a similar variant
2. Rename to reflect new axis values (`Size=Large` → `Size=XL`)
3. Resize / modify as needed
4. `componentSet.appendChild(newVariant)` — Figma auto-extends the variant axis

To remove variants: `variant.remove()`. The axis updates automatically.

**Important**: `appendChild` extends the variant AXIS but does NOT reposition the new variant inside the set's visual bounds. See Gotcha #26 for the orphaned-variant failure mode this creates. ALSO see Gotcha #27 — `clone()` may not carry forward variable bindings cleanly; re-bind after every clone.

## Layout & coordinate gotchas

### Gotcha #15 — SECTION.appendChild reinterprets x/y as section-relative
```javascript
page.appendChild(screen); screen.x = 80; screen.y = 80;  // page coords
section.appendChild(screen);                               // x/y now treated as section-relative
// To compensate, re-assign:
screen.x = 80; screen.y = 80;  // section-relative
```

### Gotcha #16 — Screen scroll pattern
- Screen frame: fixed `width/height` + `clipsContent = true`
- Chrome (Status Bar, Bottom Nav, Home Indicator) at END of children list
- `numberOfFixedChildren` counts from the END — set to count of chrome elements
- `overflowDirection = "VERTICAL"` when content overflows, `"NONE"` otherwise
- `scrollBehavior` property does NOT exist in current Plugin API

### Gotcha #18 — Auto-layout pages reorder by child index, not y-coord
The Changelog page (744:2) has VERTICAL auto-layout. Setting `child.y` won't stick — auto-layout overrides it. Use `parent.insertChild(index, node)` to control position.

For newest-first ordering on Changelog: insert new version at index 1 (after Hero at index 0).

### Gotcha #22 — layoutSizingHorizontal requires specific conditions
`node.layoutSizingHorizontal = "FILL"` fails with "node must be an auto-layout frame or a child of an auto-layout frame" when:
- The parent isn't auto-layout
- The node isn't yet appended to a parent
- The parent's `counterAxisSizingMode` is `"AUTO"` (HUG) — FILL doesn't make sense there

When unsure, use explicit `node.resize(w, h)` instead of relying on layoutSizingHorizontal.

## Audit / Bind gotchas

### Gotcha #19 — Ad cards / promotional cards on dashboard are RECTANGLE, not FRAME
Generic audit filters like `node.type === "FRAME"` miss them. To catch all background shapes:
```javascript
const allShapes = node.findAll(n => 
  (n.type === "FRAME" || n.type === "RECTANGLE") && 
  Array.isArray(n.fills) && n.fills.length > 0
);
```

### Gotcha #20 — Instance layout caches until republish
When APS spec changes (e.g., Size=Large slot 28→32 in v0.7), consumer file instances still show OLD dimensions. To force refresh:
1. APS designer publishes (UI action)
2. Consumer file: `instance.swapComponent(sameComponent)` to re-sync layout
3. Re-apply any property overrides that may have reset

### Gotcha #21 — swapComponent resets vector fill bindings
After `innerIconInstance.swapComponent(newIcon)`, the vector fills inside the new instance carry their main component's default bindings (typically Icon/Default/Default), NOT the previous instance's overrides. **Always rebind vector fills after swap:**
```javascript
innerIcon.swapComponent(newIcon);
bindIconVectors(innerIcon, tokens.iconWarning);  // re-bind required
```

## Variable / Token gotchas

### Gotcha #11 — Variable binding requires fallback color
`figma.variables.setBoundVariableForPaint` needs a fallback paint. Read the variable's value via `valuesByMode[modeId]` first:
```javascript
const modeId = Object.keys(variable.valuesByMode)[0];
const val = variable.valuesByMode[modeId];
const fallback = (val && val.r !== undefined) ? val : { r: 0, g: 0, b: 0 };
node.fills = [figma.variables.setBoundVariableForPaint(
  { type: "SOLID", color: fallback }, "color", variable
)];
```

For ALIAS variables (Semantic → Tokens → Primitives), `valuesByMode` returns `{ type: "VARIABLE_ALIAS", id: "..." }` — won't have `r/g/b`. Use a sensible fallback color literal.

### Gotcha #12 — Style keys differ from variant keys
A text style's `key` is the short hash before the comma in `style.id`. The full `style.id` is like `S:8e3f252ac204fdc99a1230bf7b5e178f5fe7083b,` — strip the prefix and comma when noting the key elsewhere.

### Gotcha #13 — importComponentByKeyAsync fails on truncated keys
Always copy the FULL component/style key (40 hex chars). Truncated keys produce "not found" errors. Copy from a freshly-fetched inventory, not from memory.

## Gotcha #24 — Centered text in flex auto-layout needs anchor frames on both sides

When you have a 3-element layout (left action | center text | right action) and you want to toggle either side off, auto-layout will collapse the hidden side and shift the center text. The title appears to "jump" between toggle states.

**Solution**: wrap each side action in a fixed-size anchor frame and bind the Show toggle to the inner element, not the anchor. The anchor stays as an invisible spacer regardless of toggle state, preserving the center position.

**Used in**: Top Nav State=Screen v0.8 — Leading Anchor (44×44) and Trailing Anchor (44×44) hold their footprint while Show Leading / Show Trailing toggle only the button/icon inside.

**Reusable for**: bottom sheet headers, modal headers, any nav bar with optional side actions, any header where centered content must stay centered regardless of which side actions are visible.

**Pattern**:
```
Container (horizontal, primary=SPACE_BETWEEN, items=CENTER)
├── Left Anchor (FRAME, fixed 44×44, transparent fill) ← always present
│   └── Left Action (toggleable visibility) ← bind Show toggle HERE, not to anchor
├── Center Content (layoutGrow=1, text-align=CENTER)
└── Right Anchor (FRAME, fixed 44×44, transparent fill) ← always present
    └── Right Action (toggleable visibility) ← bind Show toggle HERE, not to anchor
```

## Gotcha #25 — Node-ID from URL ≠ component node

A Figma URL like `?node-id=462-2` often resolves to the PAGE containing the component, not the component itself. `figma_get_component` returns `type: "CANVAS"` in this case, with the actual `COMPONENT_SET` listed in `children`. Always inspect the returned `type` before assuming you're on the target node.

**Symptom**: tool calls return metadata about a page (with children like `[Page: List, Subheader, List Item]`) when you expected a single component's properties/variants.

**Defensive read pattern**:
```javascript
const node = await figma_get_component({ nodeId: urlNodeId });
if (node.component.type === "CANVAS") {
  // We were given a page, not a component — find the real target
  const set = node.component.children.find(c => c.type === "COMPONENT_SET");
  const comp = node.component.children.find(c => c.type === "COMPONENT");
  return set?.id || comp?.id;  // retry with real component ID
}
```

**Applies to**: any read tool that accepts an arbitrary node-id from a URL the user pasted. Especially common when users copy URLs from the canvas after selecting a frame whose ID happens to match the page header.

## Gotcha #26 — Variants outside component_set bounds break the variant picker

When a variant is created via clone-and-modify (Gotcha #8 path) but ends up positioned outside the parent COMPONENT_SET's bounding box, Figma's variant picker UI suppresses or fails to switch to it — even though:
- REST API reports it as a valid child of the set
- The Type axis (or whichever variant axis) includes its value
- `figma_get_component_image` renders it correctly when called directly on the variant's node ID

**Symptom**: instance properties panel shows the variant dropdown but selecting the orphaned variant value either has no effect or appears greyed out. From a user's perspective, "the variant doesn't show up" — but structurally it exists.

**Diagnosis**: compare `absoluteBoundingBox` of each variant against the parent set's bounds. Any variant whose `x`/`y` falls outside the set's box is the culprit.

```javascript
const set = figma.getNodeById(setId);
const setBox = set.absoluteBoundingBox;
const orphans = set.children.filter(v => {
  const b = v.absoluteBoundingBox;
  return b.x < setBox.x || b.x > setBox.x + setBox.width
      || b.y < setBox.y || b.y > setBox.y + setBox.height;
});
```

**Fix**: reposition the variant inside the set's frame. Standard convention is vertical stack with ~16px gap between variants. Coordinates are relative to the set:

```javascript
const set = figma.getNodeById(setId);
let yCursor = 0;
for (const v of set.children) {
  v.x = 0;
  v.y = yCursor;
  yCursor += v.height + 16;
}
```

After repositioning, the set's bounds auto-recalculate to contain all variants. **Republish required** for consumer files (Gotcha #4 + #20).

**Real-world example**: APS v0.8 List Item — `Type=Rich` variant (1406:20) was created at canvas position (2990, 15), well outside the component_set (1406:25) bounds at (1482, -237). REST API confirmed the variant existed; Type axis included "Rich"; `figma_get_component_image` rendered it correctly. But the variant picker in instance properties either hid it or refused to switch to it. Fix: moved Rich to (1482, -156), inside the set bounds, then republished. Variant became selectable.

## Gotcha #27 — Binding completeness must be audited, not assumed ⭐ NEW v2.2

When building a component programmatically, every visual property silently falls back to a literal value if you don't explicitly bind it to a variable or style. The component looks correct in the canvas but ships as drift: designers see hard-coded values in their inspector, downstream tooling (Code Connect, Style Dictionary exports, tokens audits) can't trace the intent, and any future token change won't propagate.

**The trap is invisible.** A component built with `node.cornerRadius = 16` renders identically to one built with `node.setBoundVariable("topLeftRadius", borderRadiusXlg)` where the token's value is 16. Visual parity disguises binding absence.

**Properties that need explicit binding** (none are inferred from values):

- **Fills**: `node.fills[i]` must wrap the paint in `figma.variables.setBoundVariableForPaint`
- **Strokes**: same pattern, applied to `node.strokes[i]`
- **Stroke weights**: `strokeTopWeight`, `strokeRightWeight`, `strokeBottomWeight`, `strokeLeftWeight` (NOT `topStrokeWeight` — the API field names are inconsistent with the radius pattern)
- **Corner radii**: `topLeftRadius`, `topRightRadius`, `bottomLeftRadius`, `bottomRightRadius` (NOT `radiusTopLeft`-style — the API field names are inconsistent with the stroke pattern)
- **Auto-layout spacing**: `paddingTop/Right/Bottom/Left`, `itemSpacing`, `counterAxisSpacing`
- **Text styles**: `await textNode.setTextStyleIdAsync(style.id)` — without this, the text node has `fontName`, `fontSize`, `lineHeight`, `letterSpacing` as raw literals even though visually it matches a known style

**`clone()` does NOT carry forward `setBoundVariable` calls reliably.** When cloning a component to create variants, the cloned node may have the literal *value* but lose the variable *binding*. After every clone, re-bind anything the variant uses. Verified during APS Input Amount build — Default variant had `topLeftRadius` bound to `Border-Radius/xlg`; after `clone()`, the cloned Focused/Error/Disabled variants had `topLeftRadius = 16` as a literal, not bound. Required explicit re-binding.

**Mandatory: run an audit script after build, before publish.** Walk every node, check `node.boundVariables[prop]` exists for every visible property. The audit catches what the eye can't see:

```javascript
// Returns array of unbound visible properties on a node
function auditBindings(node) {
  const bv = node.boundVariables || {};
  const issues = [];

  // Visible fills
  (node.fills || []).forEach((f, i) => {
    if (f.type === "SOLID" && !f.boundVariables?.color) {
      issues.push(`unbound fill[${i}]`);
    }
  });

  // Visible strokes
  (node.strokes || []).forEach((s, i) => {
    if (s.type === "SOLID" && !s.boundVariables?.color) {
      issues.push(`unbound stroke[${i}]`);
    }
  });

  // Auto-layout spacing (only if node uses auto-layout)
  if (node.layoutMode && node.layoutMode !== "NONE") {
    for (const p of ["paddingTop","paddingBottom","paddingLeft","paddingRight","itemSpacing"]) {
      if (node[p] > 0 && !bv[p]) issues.push(`unbound ${p}=${node[p]}`);
    }
  }

  // Corner radii (only if non-zero)
  for (const p of ["topLeftRadius","topRightRadius","bottomLeftRadius","bottomRightRadius"]) {
    if (node[p] > 0 && !bv[p]) issues.push(`unbound ${p}=${node[p]}`);
  }

  // Stroke weights (only if strokes actually exist and are visible)
  if ((node.strokes || []).some(s => s.visible !== false)) {
    for (const p of ["strokeTopWeight","strokeRightWeight","strokeBottomWeight","strokeLeftWeight"]) {
      if (node[p] > 0 && !bv[p]) issues.push(`unbound ${p}=${node[p]}`);
    }
  }

  // Text styles
  if (node.type === "TEXT" && !node.textStyleId) {
    issues.push(`no textStyleId`);
  }

  return issues;
}

// Walk a component_set recursively
function auditTree(root) {
  const findings = [];
  function visit(n, p) {
    const issues = auditBindings(n);
    if (issues.length) findings.push({ path: p, id: n.id, name: n.name, issues });
    if (n.children) for (const c of n.children) visit(c, `${p} > ${c.name}`);
  }
  visit(root, root.name);
  return findings;
}

// Usage: target zero findings before publish
const findings = auditTree(figma.getNodeById(setId));
if (findings.length > 0) throw new Error(`${findings.length} unbound elements — fix before publish`);
```

**False positives to filter**: nodes that *have* the property API but no visible rendering of it. A frame with no strokes still has `strokeTopWeight: 1` as a default — don't flag it. Only flag stroke weights on nodes where `(node.strokes || []).some(s => s.visible !== false)`.

**Real-world example**: APS v0.9 Input Amount build. After completing all 4 variants and assuming bindings were applied correctly, an audit pass revealed 20 unbound elements: text styles missing on 16 text nodes (Label, Currency, Value across all variants — initial build set raw `fontName/fontSize` instead of `setTextStyleIdAsync`), and 12 corner-radius literals on 3 cloned variants (Default had bound radii; Focused/Error/Disabled clones lost the bindings through `clone()`). All caught by the audit, all fixable in one batched execute call. Without the audit they would have shipped as silent drift.

**Classify findings — don't collapse to one number (v2.4, 2026-06-23 re-audit).** A whole-system re-audit showed a flat `findings.length` over-reports: a COMPONENT_SET's *root + wrapper-chrome* (set-level `cornerRadius`, the 40px set padding, the set's selection border) never ships, and numeric literals are a different severity than unbound colors. Split into three buckets and gate on the first:
1. **Unbound COLOR paints** — visible SOLID fills/strokes with no `boundVariables`. The real gate; **target zero** before publish.
2. **Numeric spacing** — `paddingT/R/B/L` + `itemSpacing` literals; should bind to `Scale/*`. Flag as P2, not a publish-blocker (e.g. List Item / Dialog / Progress / Bot Nav spacing, surfaced 2026-06-23).
3. **Stroke-weight literals** — bind to `Border-Width/*` unless a sanctioned exception (e.g. the Dot outline's 1.5px — no 1.5 token exists).
Exclude the set root: in `visit()`, skip `auditBindings` when `n.type === 'COMPONENT_SET'` (and its direct wrapper-chrome). Conflating these falsely reported ~40 "defects" in the 2026-06-23 sweep when the unbound-**color** count was actually 0.

## Gotcha #28 — Component-property references detach on clone/swap; defaults are SHARED across variants ⭐ NEW v2.3

Building or extending a variant SET via the Plugin API has three traps that bite component-property wiring. All surfaced during the List Item v0.9 work (Three Line variant + icon/image swap + editable text). **Consider these for every future component creation/update.**

1. **`clone()` detaches property references.** A cloned variant's text nodes lose `componentPropertyReferences.characters`; swap nodes lose `.mainComponent`. Symptom: the new variant's text isn't editable and its swaps don't work. **Fix:** after cloning, RE-BIND every reference — `node.componentPropertyReferences = { characters: "Title#…" }` or `{ mainComponent: "Leading Icon#…" }`. (List Item Three Line: Title/Description detached after cloning Rich — only Eyebrow stayed editable until re-bound.)

2. **A property's default is SHARED across the whole set — there are NO per-variant defaults.** TEXT and INSTANCE_SWAP props have one `defaultValue` for all variants bound to them. Setting a bound node's `.characters`, or `swapComponent()`-ing it, sets that single shared default → it propagates to EVERY variant on the same prop. You CANNOT make Default default to an icon while Rich defaults to an image via one `Leading Icon` prop. (List Item: setting Three Line Title="MERALCO" changed Rich's title too; swapping one variant's leading changed all three.) **Design for uniform defaults** (one leading default, one trailing default, generic placeholder text) — variants differ via per-instance overrides, not per-variant defaults. Use separate props only if you truly need different defaults (it clutters the panel).

3. **Setting a bound node's value PRESERVES the binding** (it sets the shared default; it does NOT detach). Order matters: re-bind FIRST, then set the default value — never the reverse.

**Audit (extends Gotcha #27):** before publish, for every variant confirm each text node has a `characters` ref (editable), each swap slot has a `mainComponent` ref (swappable), each toggled node has a `visible` ref — in addition to fill/style/radius bindings. Target zero `NONE`. Walk each variant, collect `componentPropertyReferences` per text/instance/toggled node, flag any missing.

## Honest scope reminders

- **Always render-verify after any visual change** via `figma_get_component_image` or `figma_capture_screenshot`
- **Audit instances skip their internals** — bindings inside a APS instance are the APS component's responsibility. Only audit OVERRIDES at the instance level.
- **Hardcoded #FFFFFF and #000000 are usually acceptable** (no semantic token for "pure white screen" or "pure black overlay" — these are intentional)
- **Cloned dashboards in Tips screens go stale** when the source dashboard changes. Refresh by re-cloning if you've made downstream changes.
- **REST API bounds can lag plugin-side moves** — after repositioning a variant inside a set, the parent set's `bounds` field from `figma_get_component` may still report the pre-move dimensions until the file fully refreshes server-side. Confirm via the variant's own `absoluteBoundingBox` from `figma_get_component_for_development`.
- **`figma_capture_screenshot` can hang the Desktop Bridge after recent write ops** — observed during v0.9 Input Amount build. Use `figma_get_component_image` (REST endpoint) as the verification fallback, or wait a few seconds after writes before screenshotting.
- **Run the binding audit (Gotcha #27) before every publish.** It's cheap insurance against shipping silent drift.

## Quick command reference

```javascript
// Find a component_set by name
const set = figma.root.findOne(n => n.type === "COMPONENT_SET" && n.name === "Icon Button");

// Get all variants
const variants = set.children.filter(c => c.type === "COMPONENT");

// Find a specific variant
const tertiary = set.children.find(c => 
  c.name === "Appearance=Tertiary, Type=Filled, Size=Default, State=Default"
);

// Audit a node tree, skipping instances
function audit(node, callback) {
  if (node.type === "INSTANCE") return;
  callback(node);
  if ("children" in node && node.children) {
    for (const c of node.children) audit(c, callback);
  }
}

// Bind a fill to a variable
function bindFill(node, variable) {
  const modeId = Object.keys(variable.valuesByMode)[0];
  const val = variable.valuesByMode[modeId];
  const fallback = (val && val.r !== undefined) ? val : { r: 0, g: 0, b: 0 };
  node.fills = [figma.variables.setBoundVariableForPaint(
    { type: "SOLID", color: fallback }, "color", variable
  )];
}

// Recursively bind icon vectors
function bindIconVectors(node, variable) {
  if (node.type === "VECTOR" && node.fills?.length > 0 && node.name !== "Bounding box") {
    bindFill(node, variable);
  }
  if (node.type === "RECTANGLE" && node.name === "Bounding box") {
    node.fills = [];  // clear hit-target rect
  }
  if ("children" in node && node.children) {
    for (const c of node.children) bindIconVectors(c, variable);
  }
}

// Detect orphaned variants in a component_set (Gotcha #26)
function findOrphanedVariants(setId) {
  const set = figma.getNodeById(setId);
  const setBox = set.absoluteBoundingBox;
  return set.children.filter(v => {
    const b = v.absoluteBoundingBox;
    return b.x < setBox.x || b.x + b.width > setBox.x + setBox.width
        || b.y < setBox.y || b.y + b.height > setBox.y + setBox.height;
  }).map(v => ({ id: v.id, name: v.name, x: v.x, y: v.y }));
}

// Restack variants vertically inside a set (Gotcha #26 fix)
function restackVariants(setId, gap = 16) {
  const set = figma.getNodeById(setId);
  let yCursor = 0;
  for (const v of set.children) {
    v.x = 0;
    v.y = yCursor;
    yCursor += v.height + gap;
  }
}
```

## Gotcha #29 — `getVariableByIdAsync` needs the `VariableID:` prefix; a bare id returns null ⭐ NEW v2.4

`figma.variables.getVariableByIdAsync('23:62')` returns **`null`** — the API expects the full form **`'VariableID:23:62'`**. The trap: `node.setBoundVariable(prop, null)` (and `setBoundVariableForPaint(..., null)`) **silently no-ops** — no throw, no error — so a binding pass that used bare ids reports success while binding *nothing*. The empty `boundVariables` only surfaces on an independent re-read.

**WRONG**
```js
const v = await figma.variables.getVariableByIdAsync('23:62'); // → null
node.setBoundVariable('paddingTop', v);                        // silent no-op
```
**CORRECT**
```js
const v = await figma.variables.getVariableByIdAsync('VariableID:23:62'); // resolves
node.setBoundVariable('paddingTop', v);
```

**Always** prefix variable ids with `VariableID:` (the manifest stores them this way in `keyTokens`), and **re-read `boundVariables` after binding** to confirm it took — never trust the absence of an error. Bit the 2026-06-23 spacing-tokenization pass (a whole bind round reported false success), and earlier when a bare `'23:5'` lookup returned null during the Dot build.

